-- title:
--   Constant term gives a ℤ-point of the pole chart
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a positive natural number and let $F = \mathbb{Q}\bigl(\,j(q^d) : d \mid N,\ d \neq 0\,\bigr)$ be the intermediate field `modularFunctionFieldFull N` of the field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ \mathbb{Q}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, generated by the $q$-expansions `qExpand ℚ d jq` for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$; write $j \in F$ for the element `jFull N`, that is, the series $jq$ regarded as an element of $F$. Let $A_\infty =$ `chartAlgInf ℤ F (jFull N)` be the subalgebra of $F$ consisting of those elements that are integral over the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{Z}[j^{-1}] =$ `Algebra.adjoin ℤ {j⁻¹}` of $F$. The assertion is that there exists a homomorphism $\varphi \colon A_\infty \to \mathbb{Z}$ of $\mathbb{Z}$-algebras such that for every $x \in A_\infty$ the image of $\varphi(x)$ under the inclusion $\mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{Q}$ equals the coefficient of $q^0$ of the Laurent series underlying $x$; that is, $\varphi$ reads off the constant term of the $q$-expansion, and in particular that constant term is an integer for every $x \in A_\infty$.
--
--   This exhibits the cusp at infinity as a $\mathbb{Z}$-point of the chart at infinity of the two-chart integral model of the modular curve: evaluation at the cusp, i.e. taking the constant term of the $q$-expansion, is a $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra homomorphism on the algebra of functions integral over $\mathbb{Z}[1/j]$. It is the instance-free $\mathbb{Z}$-form used in the construction of the Deligne–Rapoport-style model packages, being cited in the production of sections over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$ and in the separation of cusps on the fibres.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_IgusaScheme_exists_algHom_int_chartAlgInf_eq_coeff_zero.lean

import Mathlib
